<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sati</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as suttee, sahagamana, sahamarana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sati, or suttee, is a chiefly historical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> practice in which a widow burns alive on her dead husband's funeral pyre, voluntarily, by coercion, or for lack of acceptable options for living on. Greek sources of around 300 BCE mention it, Hindu inscriptions record it from 464 CE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> law-books from the 7th century; by the 12th century it was widely recognised, though not universally accepted. In the Mughal period it was associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> elites. After campaigns by missionaries such as William Carey and reformers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ram-mohan-roy">Raja Ram Mohan Roy</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> Sati Regulation, 1829, made it punishable, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s Sati (Prevention) Act, 1987, criminalised aiding or glorifying it.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…devotional song, and some of the greatest bhakti poets, such as Mirabai and the Tamil saint Andal, were women; women also faced restrictions in the classical law books, and practices such as the burning of widows, <b>sati</b>, were opposed by reformers and banned under British rule in 1829.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1857 the British avoided intervening in religion, even over the remarriage of Hindu child widows, though <b>sati</b> had been banned before the rebellion; Indian reformers took up the cause, from Pandita Ramabai, who championed the remarriage of Brahmin widows, to Gokhale’s Servants of India Society.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>Tomé Pires described the lords of Java going out with large retinues and keeping apartments for their women, and Ma Huan recorded that among the upper class widowed wives, concubines or female servants performed <b>sati</b>, throwing themselves into the flames of the cremation fire.</blockquote><a href="/societies/majapahit">Social Hierarchy of Majapahit</a></li><li><blockquote>Among Hindus, <b>sati</b>, a widow’s burning on her husband’s pyre, was practised in some families, and Aurangzeb ordered it banned in 1663.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mughal-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mughal Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>It defeated the Kingdom of Mysore, the Marathas and the Sikh Empire, bound princely states by subsidiary alliances, imposed the Permanent Settlement of 1793, abolished <b>sati</b> in 1829 and introduced English education.</blockquote><a href="/state/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India</a></li></ul></section>
